# Sorting in Go

Go's `sort` standard library package provides a range of functions for sorting slices and collections. Go uses **pdqsort** (pattern-defeating quicksort), a highly efficient algorithm.

## Sorting Primitive Types

```go
import "sort"

// sort.Ints — sorts []int ascending
nums := []int{5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3}
sort.Ints(nums)
fmt.Println(nums) // [1 2 3 5 8 9]

// sort.Strings — sorts []string lexicographically
words := []string{"banana", "apple", "mango"}
sort.Strings(words)
fmt.Println(words) // [apple banana mango]

// sort.Float64s
prices := []float64{3.14, 1.41, 2.71}
sort.Float64s(prices)
fmt.Println(prices) // [1.41 2.71 3.14]
```

## sort.Slice — Custom Comparator

```go
type Person struct { Name string; Age int }
people := []Person{{"Charlie", 30}, {"Alice", 25}, {"Bob", 35}}

sort.Slice(people, func(i, j int) bool {
    return people[i].Age < people[j].Age
})
// [{Alice 25} {Charlie 30} {Bob 35}]
```

## sort.SliceStable — Preserves Relative Order

```go
sort.SliceStable(data, func(i, j int) bool {
    return data[i].Score > data[j].Score
})
// Equal-score elements maintain their original relative order
```

## sort.Search — Binary Search

```go
nums := []int{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11}
i := sort.Search(len(nums), func(i int) bool {
    return nums[i] >= 7
})
fmt.Println(nums[i]) // 7
```

## Reverse Sort

```go
// Using sort.Slice with reversed condition
data := []int{3, 1, 4, 1, 5}
sort.Slice(data, func(i, j int) bool {
    return data[i] > data[j] // > for descending
})
fmt.Println(data) // [5 4 3 1 1]
```

starterCode:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

type Student struct {
	Name  string
	Score int
	Grade string
}

func main() {
	// Sort primitive types
	nums := []int{64, 25, 12, 22, 11}
	sort.Ints(nums)
	fmt.Println("Sorted ints:", nums)

	words := []string{"banana", "apple", "cherry", "date"}
	sort.Strings(words)
	fmt.Println("Sorted strings:", words)

	// Custom sort with sort.Slice
	students := []Student{
		{"Charlie", 85, "B"},
		{"Alice", 92, "A"},
		{"Bob", 85, "B"},
		{"Dave", 78, "C"},
	}

	// Sort by score descending, then name ascending
	sort.SliceStable(students, func(i, j int) bool {
		if students[i].Score != students[j].Score {
			return students[i].Score > students[j].Score
		}
		return students[i].Name < students[j].Name
	})

	fmt.Println("\nSorted students:")
	for _, s := range students {
		fmt.Printf("  %s: %d (%s)\n", s.Name, s.Score, s.Grade)
	}

	// Binary search
	sortedNums := []int{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15}
	target := 7
	idx := sort.Search(len(sortedNums), func(i int) bool {
		return sortedNums[i] >= target
	})
	if idx < len(sortedNums) && sortedNums[idx] == target {
		fmt.Printf("\nFound %d at index %d\n", target, idx)
	}

	// Check if sorted
	fmt.Println("Is sorted:", sort.IntsAreSorted(sortedNums))
}
